d  episodes into tv movies  later tv distribution allowed the show  to be available worldwide  by the time the finales were completed  about  800 episodes hit the airwaves the show  csi las vegas miami  new york and cyber  each episode had key features that led  to its continued success  it all started with a crime or a series  of crimes  the csis arrived on scene in their  hummer and gathered evidence  typically in plain clothes the evidence  was returned to a state-of-the-art crime  lab full of future-esque  instrumentation the government labs  typically didn't have or couldn't afford  by the end of the hour-long episode they  had analyzed all of the evidence  gathered  solved the crime and made arrests  the show created long-lasting effects  for crime labs  investigators and prosecutors as we  tried to explain to juries of the  obstacles we have in testing every piece  of evidence  while struggling with rising backlogs  increased caseloads and the inability to  purchase some of the cutting-edge  instrumentation  presented in the show here's a brief  background  about arizona our state currently has a  population of about 7.6 million people  the department of public safety provides  lab services to more than 200 agencies  across 15 counties throughout our state  we also ranked 10th in violent crime  according to the 2018  fbi uniform crime reporting program  so what is allowed to do facing  increased dna analysis requests  and large backlogs  our lab superintendent developed an  operational vision of how our lab could  help assist investigations  and forge lasting partnerships with our  user agencies through the use of rapid  dna  if we could provide world-class customer  service and utilize emerging technology  we could also meet part of our mission  for the technical support division  at dps in june of 2013  our lab purchased rapid dna instruments  and a group of three people were tasked  with validating the instrument to  determine  if it met certain requirements for use  once it passed the validation we created  a training program and implemented a law  enforcement dna program  through validation we were able to  determine that we could process single  source high quantity dna samples  while this limited some of the case  types and evidence we could use we were  still able to process homicide  aggravated assault and property crimes  each of the samples  processed through our rapid program are  submitted to an accredited crime  laboratory for confirmation  furthermore the operators cannot consume  any of the evidence as a part of this  program  in addition we created a standalone  database for the program  so operators could search for possible  candidates when a suspect standard was  not available  i can't cover every detail of our  program today so we can go over that in  a future talk  a lot of time was spent developing a  training program  and looking to see which agencies would  be interested in being a part of our  program  we offered it at no cost to the agencies  that were interested  and several agencies were ready to sign  up  in may of 2014 we conducted our first  training class  we trained 10 operators during an  extensive 40-hour  hour class after  waiting and waiting in what seemed like  forever  the first case came in july of 2014  i'd like to go over a quick recap of  some of our success  our program we have trained over 80  operators from 18  agencies if you remember from earlier we  service over 200 agencies  through dps so that's why our program  requires each operator to assist other  agencies and process those cases  so we can extend the impact of our  program  our operators have processed 531 cases  and aided 175 investigations  the program has even ruled out  investigative leads in some cases which  has allowed the subject to return to  their life and not be the focus of a  criminal investigation  the investigative weeds generated  equates to a 33  hit rate i'd like to go through a few  case examples to illustrate the variety  of cases  and the impact to our community  the first case began during a traffic  stop in the middle of the night  the officer was verifying the  information from the driver and four  other occupants during this time one of  the occupants shot the officer in the  face  while backup was already on the way the  suspect car circled around a building to  return more fire  the backup officer arrived on scene and  additional gun  gunfire was exchanged between the  parties the car was found abandoned  early the next morning with several  bullet holes there was blood in the rear  seat of the car where the suspect who  had the gun had been sitting  near the car was a bloody shirt with a  hole through it  items were tested with rapid dna and the  dna profile obtained hit to a person in  our standalone database  they now knew who they were looking for  and found him about a week later  a jury found him guilty on seven charges  and he received a long sentence  the next case is a bank robbery from  southern arizona  the man you see in the slide walked into  a wells fargo bank  and made demands on a sheet of paper  indicating he had a weapon  after having his demands met he ran out  of the bank but left the sunglasses you  see  behind the sunglasses were tested and  the profile hit to a person in our  standalone database  police were able to apprehend him and he  was found guilty on felony robbery  charges  the next case is a child molestation  case out of the east valley  a family friend was in town for bike  week which is a motorcycle convention  that has concerts and rides  where some of the proceeds go to local  charities  the homeowners and their children went  to bed for the night and then the family  friend was found wandering the hallway  in the early morning when else what he  was doing  he said he was looking for a towel for a  shower  later that morning one of the kids told  their mother that someone was in their  room in the middle of the 